        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(slack): support Enterprise Grid org installs",
        "body": "Closes #102371\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nSlack Enterprise Grid org installations can deliver events for multiple granted workspaces through one bot token. OpenClaw previously compared every event with one startup workspace and used detached clients later, so valid events from other granted workspaces could be rejected or replied to without a durable workspace boundary.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis adds an explicit `enterpriseOrgInstall` Slack account mode. Startup validates the org installation with `auth.test`; each direct `message` or `app_mention` uses Bolt's validated enterprise, app, workspace, and listener-owned `WebClient`; sessions, routes, transcripts, dedupe keys, and Slack caches are workspace-qualified.\n\nEnterprise V1 deliberately fails closed for relay delivery, proactive/deferred/queued delivery, runtime bindings, Slack action tools, and Slack-native approvals. Existing workspace installations keep their current startup, client, action, binding, and delivery behavior.\n\n## User Impact\n\nEnterprise Grid operators can run one org-installed Slack app across every workspace Slack has granted to that installation, while keeping message authorization and runtime state isolated per workspace. Operators enable the mode explicitly and follow the documented Socket Mode or HTTP org-install workflow.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Added startup identity tests for config/token mismatches, canonical Slack IDs, unsupported policy surfaces, and workspace-mode compatibility.\n- Added two-workspace event-scope tests, wrong-app/wrong-enterprise/missing-team rejection, bot-event rejection, and MPDM policy normalization.\n- Added listener-client identity and no-network Slack SDK proof that Bolt's team-scoped `WebClient` automatically injects `team_id`.\n- Added enterprise immediate-reply tests for text/media, chunking, retries, receipts, unfurl policy, and custom identity fallback.\n- Added fail-closed tests for direct sends, actions, native approvals, runtime bindings, live queue admission, queue recovery, and conditional queue transitions.\n- Initial focused proof passed 191 core outbound tests and 165 Slack tests before the local dependency snapshot drifted.\n- Final staged checks passed: `git diff --check`, oxfmt on all changed files, oxlint, generated channel metadata check, MDX/manifest validation, Slack extension `tsgo`, independent verification, and repository autoreview with zero actionable findings.\n- The follow-up org-token compatibility fix passed 62 focused Slack tests plus the Slack package runtime build and the repository QA runtime build.\n- Root `tsgo` has no diagnostics in staged paths; its remaining diagnostics are unchanged `extensions/qa-lab` Crabline errors.\n\nLive Enterprise Grid org-install proof (redacted, 2026-07-08 PDT, rebased head `392c410686f`):\n\n```text\nauth.test: ok=true, is_enterprise_install=true,\n           enterprise_id=<redacted>, app_id=<omitted by Slack>\ngateway:   slack Enterprise Grid org-install mode enabled\ngateway:   slack socket mode connected\ninbound:   app_mention slack:<workspace-redacted>:channel:<redacted>:user:<redacted>\n           -> bot:<redacted> (channel, 144 chars)\nrequest:   Reply in this thread with exactly:\n           CORPORATE_WORKSPACE_OK 20260708-223327-PDT\nreply:     CORPORATE_WORKSPACE_OK 20260708-223327-PDT\n```\n\nThe request was sent by a human Slack user in a granted Enterprise Grid workspace. The local gateway received it through Socket Mode, preserved the event workspace context, and democlaw posted the exact response in the originating thread. No token, private endpoint, IP address, or internal Slack identifier is included above.\n\nManual verification:\n\n- [x] Validate two workspaces under one enterprise produce distinct scoped clients and session/cache identities.\n- [x] Validate wrong enterprise, wrong app, missing workspace, mutable-name policy, and unsupported delivery surfaces fail closed.\n- [x] Validate workspace-install behavior remains covered by regression tests.\n- [x] Parse and validate least-privilege Enterprise Grid Socket Mode and HTTP manifests.\n- [x] Run a live Enterprise Grid org-install message/reply smoke test in an operator-owned workspace.\n",
        "branch": "dev/kevinlin/slack-enterprise-org-routing",
